  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The formula to calculate the kinetic energy can be written as</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced></math></p><p>Since oxygen is a diatomic gas, the number of degrees of freedom is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn></math>.</p><p>Thus, from equation (1), it follows that</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtable columnalign=\"right center left\" columnspacing=\"0px\"><mtr><mtd><mi>K</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>31</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>300</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>6232</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></p>",
